- No virtualization or dynamic loading

**Rendering approach:**
- Every image tag uses the signed URL directly as `src={getImageUrl(...)}`
- All images load immediately on page render (not lazy)
- Display size is small (12-14px square), but full-resolution original is fetched

### SUMMARY: Network & Download Costs for Page with N Receipts

| Metric | Count |
|--------|-------|
| **Signed-URL network calls** | N (one per receipt, all parallelized via Promise.all) |
| **Image downloads** | N (one full-resolution image per receipt) |
| **Parallelization** | Yes, signed URLs are generated in parallel |
| **Caching** | Client-side only (in-memory Record), per session |
| **Lazy loading** | No |
| **Thumbnails** | No |
| **Resized originals stored** | Yes (pre-upload resize to 2000px max, 85% quality applied) |

**Performance bottleneck:** The main issue is that generating N signed URLs requires N sequential Supabase API calls (even though they execute in parallel via Promise.all, each is still an individual HTTP request). The Supabase SDK does not expose a batch `createSignedUrls()` method, so there's no way to reduce this to a single request. For a page with 100 receipts, you'd see ~100 signed URL HTTP requests firing concurrently on page load.

