Here's the proposed code for your review **before** I write anything. Two pieces — a pure, injectable core and a thin Supabase wrapper.

**The seam I'm proposing** (in the common util dir, per your (a)):

```ts
// src/lib/utils/signedUrl.ts  — additions alongside the existing getSignedReceiptUrl

/** Max paths per createSignedUrls request — bounds payload size and the blast radius of one failed batch. */
const SIGN_BATCH_SIZE = 200;

/** One entry from Supabase storage createSignedUrls. */
export interface SignedUrlResult {
	path: string | null;
	signedUrl: string | null;
	error: string | null;
}

/** The injectable I/O boundary: sign a batch of paths. */
export type BatchSigner = (paths: string[]) => Promise<SignedUrlResult[]>;

function chunk<T>(items: T[], size: number): T[][] {
	const out: T[][] = [];
	for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i += size) out.push(items.slice(i, i + size));
	return out;
}

/**
 * Pure core: dedupe + chunk paths, sign each chunk via the injected signer,
 * fold successful results into a path→url map. Soft-fails — a rejected chunk or
 * a per-item error drops only those paths and never throws.
 */
export async function collectSignedUrls(
	paths: string[],
	sign: BatchSigner,
	batchSize: number = SIGN_BATCH_SIZE
): Promise<Record<string, string>> {
	const unique = [...new Set(paths.filter((p): p is string => !!p))];
	const out: Record<string, string> = {};
	await Promise.all(
		chunk(unique, batchSize).map(async (batch) => {
			try {
				for (const r of await sign(batch)) {
					if (r.path && r.signedUrl && !r.error) out[r.path] = r.signedUrl;
				}
			} catch (e) {
				console.error('Signed URL batch failed:', e); // debug-only; blank thumbs, page survives
			}
		})
	);
	return out;
}

/** Public wrapper: wires the real Supabase storage client into the pure core. */
export async function getSignedReceiptUrls(
	paths: string[],
	expiresIn: number = DEFAULT_EXPIRY_SECONDS
): Promise<Record<string, string>> {
	return collectSignedUrls(paths, async (batch) => {
		const { data, error } = await supabase.storage.from('receipts').createSignedUrls(batch, expiresIn);
		if (error) throw error;
		return data ?? [];
	});
}
```

**The test drives only `collectSignedUrls` with a typed fake signer** — never touches Supabase (your no-live-API / no-`any` rules):

```ts
// src/lib/utils/signedUrl.spec.ts
const fakeSigner = (urls: Record<string, string>): BatchSigner =>
	async (paths) => paths.map((p) => ({ path: p, signedUrl: urls[p] ?? null, error: urls[p] ? null : 'missing' }));

// asserts: path→url mapping; dedupe; chunk boundaries (spy counts calls for 201 paths → 2 batches);
// per-item error drops only that path; a signer that throws drops only its chunk and never rejects.
```

One wrinkle I want your call on, and it's why I'm showing this first:

**`signedUrl.ts` imports the singleton `supabase` at module load.** A unit test that imports this file to reach `collectSignedUrls` will also load `$lib/supabaseClient` (which needs `PUBLIC_SUPABASE_*` env). Two ways to keep the test clean:

- **One file** (above): simplest, but the spec transitively loads `supabaseClient` — fine *if* the test env has those public env vars or the client constructs lazily. Riskier.
- **Split**: put the pure `chunk` + `collectSignedUrls` + types in a new `src/lib/utils/signedUrlBatch.ts` (zero Supabase import), and have `signedUrl.ts` import them for the wrapper. The spec imports only `signedUrlBatch.ts` → no Supabase at all. One extra file, but the pure core is hermetically testable.

My recommendation: **split into `signedUrlBatch.ts`.** It guarantees the unit test never drags in the Supabase client or env, which is exactly the isolation your "keep logic pure, no live API in tests" rule wants — and it keeps both files in the same common dir, satisfying (a).

Does the code shape look right to you, and do you want the **split** (`signedUrlBatch.ts`) or the **single-file** version?

